#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d655fc-fecd-566e-a112-395d1edf56c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339813} {:answer/id #uuid "dde6421c-a0ec-5264-8c92-9b0a637dca86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339814} {:answer/id #uuid "60d076cb-e78c-5ba9-891b-6fbb24e6b378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339815} {:answer/id #uuid "03f084cc-0963-58a0-b760-90872dd423c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339816} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bb5fe0-c4b7-539a-b5e3-526ba94a91ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde6421c-a0ec-5264-8c92-9b0a637dca86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339814} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e21619-c39f-5b4c-a291-814dec44aada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "392b0319-d12c-58e9-a1f7-7742e3726555" :question/math-academy-id "q-125457" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
